WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.137
This example demonstrates a reversible update.

00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:08.547
We begin a transaction, standardize customer emails by trimming spaces and converting the text to lowercase,

00:00:08.547 --> 00:00:13.312
and use returning to display the affected records.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:19.952
The where condition limits the update to rows where the current email is actually different from the standardized

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:21.144
version.

00:00:21.674 --> 00:00:24.252
Then the example performs a rollback.

00:00:24.252 --> 00:00:32.017
That means you can see which rows would change, but the database does not keep the changes after the transaction ends.

00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:35.826
This is an excellent way to test cleaning logic.

00:00:35.826 --> 00:00:44.367
When you are confident that the transformation is correct and appropriate, the same workflow can end with commit instead

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:44.786
of rollback.

00:00:44.786 --> 00:00:52.141
While developing or teaching, rollback gives you a repeatable example because the source data remains unchanged.
